# Bounded JWE batches in Livebook

This notebook opens real compact JWEs using `RequestSeal.JOSE.JWE.decrypt_many/2`: ordered results, item rejections, outer failures, grouping by custody handle, resolver expiry, and caller cancellation. The envelopes use [RFC 7516](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7516.html), AES key wrapping from [RFC 7518 §4.4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7518.html#section-4.4), and AES-GCM content encryption. Batching is a RequestSeal API, not a new JWE serialization. No server or network peer is required beyond the Hex install.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:request_seal, "~> 2.0"}])
```

## Check published AES key-wrap bytes

[RFC 3394 §4.3](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3394.html#section-4.3) publishes these exact wrapping-key, key-data, and wrapped bytes. They are public test material, never production credentials. Custody must unwrap them exactly before we use freshly generated keys for the batch.

```elixir
alias RequestSeal.Custody
alias RequestSeal.JOSE.JWE

published_kek =
  Base.decode16!("000102030405060708090A0B0C0D0E0F101112131415161718191A1B1C1D1E1F")

published_key = Base.decode16!("00112233445566778899AABBCCDDEEFF")
published_wrapped = Base.decode16!("64E8C3F9CE0F5BA263E9777905818A2A93C8191E7D6E8AE7")
{:ok, vector_handle} = Custody.Local.new({:jwe, "A256KW"}, {:aes, published_kek})

try do
  {:ok, [{:ok, ^published_key}]} =
    Custody.unwrap(vector_handle, [{published_wrapped, %{}, 128}])
after
  :ok = Custody.release(vector_handle)
end

IO.puts("Published AES key-wrap bytes recovered exactly")
```

## Encrypt three items using two caller-owned handles

Items carry exactly `:token` and `:expected`. Expectations are public protected-header values, checked independently for each position. The first and third items use the same handle. The resolver uses the untrusted `kid` only to look up an already trusted handle; it performs no authorization or other trusted side effect.

```elixir
{:ok, handle_a} = Custody.Local.generate({:jwe, "A256KW"}, [])
{:ok, handle_b} = Custody.Local.generate({:jwe, "A256KW"}, [])
[:wrap, :unwrap] = handle_a.capabilities
[:wrap, :unwrap] = handle_b.capabilities
handles = %{"key-a" => handle_a, "key-b" => handle_b}

items =
  for {kid, page} <- [{"key-a", 0}, {"key-b", 1}, {"key-a", 2}] do
    {:ok, token} =
      JWE.encrypt(
        [{"alg", "A256KW"}, {"enc", "A256GCM"}, {"kid", kid}, {"page", page}],
        "payload#{page}",
        Map.fetch!(handles, kid)
      )

    %{token: token, expected: %{"kid" => kid, "page" => page}}
  end

policy = %{
  algorithms: ["A256KW"],
  encryption: ["A256GCM"],
  max_plaintext: 1024,
  max_tokens: 10,
  max_total_input: 65_536,
  max_total_plaintext: 10_240,
  timeout: 5_000,
  resolve_timeout: 1000,
  unwrap_timeout: 1000,
  protected: %{permitted: []},
  key_resolver: fn %{algorithm: algorithm, header: header} ->
    case Map.fetch(handles, header["kid"]) do
      {:ok, handle} -> {:ok, %{algorithm: algorithm, key: handle}}
      :error -> :error
    end
  end
}

3 = length(items)
{:ok, [{:ok, %{plaintext: "payload0"}}, {:ok, %{plaintext: "payload1"}},
       {:ok, %{plaintext: "payload2"}}]} = JWE.decrypt_many(items, policy)
IO.puts("Three authenticated plaintexts returned in input order")
```

## Compare with single open and the empty batch

The outer `{:ok, results}` means the batch has a final positional list; individual entries still decide success or failure. The single-item policy puts expectations in `:protected`, while batch expectations belong to each item. The phase budgets are explicit and `resolve_timeout + unwrap_timeout` must be strictly below `timeout`.

```elixir
singles =
  Enum.map(items, fn item ->
    single_policy =
      policy
      |> Map.drop([:max_tokens, :max_total_input, :max_total_plaintext])
      |> Map.put(:protected, %{expected: item.expected, permitted: []})

    {:ok, _} = opened = JWE.decrypt(item.token, single_policy)
    opened
  end)

{:ok, ^singles} = JWE.decrypt_many(items, policy)
{:ok, []} = JWE.decrypt_many([], policy)
IO.puts("Batch equals single opens; an empty batch succeeds with an empty list")
```

## Keep item failures at their original positions

Flip a bit in the second token's real authentication tag, change an expected public value, omit a required item field, and use a reserved expectation name. Good neighbors must still decrypt, with every rejection at its own position. No failed entry carries plaintext.

```elixir
[first, middle, last] = items
[protected, encrypted_key, iv, ciphertext, tag] = String.split(middle.token, ".")
<<head, tail::binary>> = Base.url_decode64!(tag, padding: false)
changed_tag = Base.url_encode64(<<Bitwise.bxor(head, 1), tail::binary>>, padding: false)
forged = %{middle | token: Enum.join([protected, encrypted_key, iv, ciphertext, changed_tag], ".")}
wrong_context = %{middle | expected: Map.put(middle.expected, "page", 99)}
malformed_item = %{token: first.token}
invalid_expectation = %{first | expected: %{"alg" => "A256KW"}}

{:ok, [
  {:ok, %{plaintext: "payload0"}},
  {:error, %{reason: :decryption_failed, layer: :crypto}},
  {:error, %{reason: :protected_mismatch, layer: :input}},
  {:error, %{reason: :invalid_serialization, layer: :input}},
  {:error, %{reason: :invalid_policy, layer: :input}},
  {:ok, %{plaintext: "payload2"}}
]} = JWE.decrypt_many([first, forged, wrong_context, malformed_item, invalid_expectation, last], policy)

IO.puts("Item rejections preserved position and both good neighbors")
```

## Decode authenticated objects at each position

Object content uses the same phase budgets and protected expectations as binary
content. The object is decoded only after authentication. A duplicate member
returns a positional decoder error with no plaintext; successful neighbors
retain both authenticated bytes and their `object` map.

```elixir
object_items =
  for bytes <- [~s({"page":0}), ~s({"page":1,"page":2})] do
    {:ok, token} = JWE.encrypt([{"alg", "A256KW"}, {"enc", "A256GCM"}, {"kid", "key-a"}], bytes, handle_a)
    %{token: token, expected: %{"kid" => "key-a"}}
  end

{:ok, [
  {:ok, %{object: %{"page" => 0}, plaintext: ~s({"page":0})}},
  {:error, object_error}
]} = JWE.decrypt_many(object_items, Map.put(policy, :content, :object))
:duplicate_member = object_error.reason
:input = object_error.layer
false = Map.has_key?(object_error, :plaintext)
false = Map.has_key?(object_error, :object)
IO.puts("Object batch retained a good neighbor and withheld malformed content")
```

[Strict JSON object content](../docs/guides/json-object-content.md) documents
all decoder bounds and the standalone `RequestSeal.JOSE.decode_object/2` API.

## Refuse outer failures before key resolution

A non-list input, invalid policy, or aggregate limit returns one outer error without a result list. Total input includes serialized expectations, and total plaintext counts ciphertext widths before key work. The resolver below delegates to the real resolver and adds a control notice so the cell can assert that outer failures never call it.

```elixir
observer = self()
outer_tag = make_ref()
resolve = policy.key_resolver

observed_policy = %{
  policy
  | key_resolver: fn request ->
      send(observer, {:outer_resolved, outer_tag})
      resolve.(request)
    end
}

{:error, %{reason: :invalid_serialization, layer: :input}} =
  JWE.decrypt_many(%{}, observed_policy)

{:error, %{reason: :invalid_policy, layer: :input}} =
  JWE.decrypt_many(items, %{observed_policy | unwrap_timeout: 4_000})

token_bytes = Enum.sum(Enum.map(items, &byte_size(&1.token)))

for limited <- [
      %{observed_policy | max_tokens: 2},
      %{observed_policy | max_total_input: token_bytes},
      %{observed_policy | max_total_plaintext: 1}
    ] do
  {:error, %{reason: :limit, layer: :input}} = JWE.decrypt_many(items, limited)
end

receive do
  {:outer_resolved, ^outer_tag} -> raise "Outer refusal reached key resolution"
after
  0 -> :ok
end

IO.puts("Outer serialization, policy, and all three aggregate limits refused before resolution")
```

## Observe one unwrap call per handle group

Attach only for this operation, then detach in `after`. The three tokens should produce two groups and custody calls of one and two items, despite the first and third tokens having different headers. These events contain counts and duration, never handles, key IDs, token bytes, or plaintext.

```elixir
defmodule NotebookBatchTelemetry do
  def handle_event(event, measurements, metadata, {destination, tag}) do
    send(destination, {:batch_event, tag, event, measurements, metadata})
  end
end

handler = make_ref()
event_tag = make_ref()

:ok =
  :telemetry.attach_many(
    handler,
    [[:request_seal, :jose, :decrypt_many], [:request_seal, :custody, :unwrap]],
    &NotebookBatchTelemetry.handle_event/4,
    {self(), event_tag}
  )

try do
  {:ok, ^singles} = JWE.decrypt_many(items, policy)

  receive do
    {:batch_event, ^event_tag, [:request_seal, :jose, :decrypt_many],
     %{count: 3, groups: 2, failed: 0, deadline_exceeded: 0}, metadata} ->
      %{} = metadata
      0 = map_size(metadata)
  after
    5_000 -> raise "Missing batch group event"
  end

  counts =
    for _ <- 1..2 do
      receive do
        {:batch_event, ^event_tag, [:request_seal, :custody, :unwrap], measurements, metadata} ->
          [:duration, :items] = Enum.sort(Map.keys(measurements))
          true = is_integer(measurements.duration) and measurements.duration >= 0
          0 = map_size(metadata)
          measurements.items
      after
        5_000 -> raise "Missing custody group event"
      end
    end

  [1, 2] = Enum.sort(counts)

  receive do
    {:batch_event, ^event_tag, _, _, _} -> raise "Unexpected extra group event"
  after
    0 -> :ok
  end
after
  :ok = :telemetry.detach(handler)
end

IO.puts("Three tokens used two handle groups and two unwrap calls")
```

## Expire one resolver while preserving its neighbors

This is an actual application resolver paused on a message, not a replacement cryptographic service. Only the middle position waits; the other two return their real custody handle. Resolution ends at the policy's R budget, producing `:key_resolver_failed` at that position and terminating the waiting resolver.

```elixir
expiry_tag = make_ref()

expiry_policy = %{
  policy
  | key_resolver: fn request ->
      if request.header["page"] == 1 do
        send(observer, {:expiry_waiting, expiry_tag, self()})

        receive do
          {:resume, ^expiry_tag} -> resolve.(request)
        end
      else
        resolve.(request)
      end
    end
}

{:ok, [
  {:ok, %{plaintext: "payload0"}},
  {:error, %{reason: :key_resolver_failed, layer: :key}},
  {:ok, %{plaintext: "payload2"}}
]} = JWE.decrypt_many(items, expiry_policy)

receive do
  {:expiry_waiting, ^expiry_tag, resolver} -> false = Process.alive?(resolver)
after
  5_000 -> raise "Resolver expiry was not exercised"
end

IO.puts("Expired resolver ended; neighboring authenticated results survived")
```

## Cancel an in-flight batch by ending its caller

Start a real batch in a monitored caller, wait for its resolver to begin, then kill the caller. Long phase budgets keep the cancellation check distinct from deadline expiry. Monitor both processes and require `:killed` exits; no batch result may escape. The notebook owns the custody handles, so cancelling this operation must leave them usable for another call.

```elixir
cancel_tag = make_ref()

cancel_policy = %{
  policy
  | timeout: 30_000,
    resolve_timeout: 20_000,
    key_resolver: fn request ->
      send(observer, {:cancel_waiting, cancel_tag, self()})

      receive do
        {:resume, ^cancel_tag} -> resolve.(request)
      end
    end
}

{caller, caller_monitor} =
  spawn_monitor(fn ->
    result = JWE.decrypt_many([first], cancel_policy)
    send(observer, {:batch_returned, cancel_tag, result})
  end)

try do
  resolver =
    receive do
      {:cancel_waiting, ^cancel_tag, pid} -> pid
    after
      5_000 -> raise "Cancellation resolver did not start"
    end

  resolver_monitor = Process.monitor(resolver)

  try do
    receive do
      {:batch_returned, ^cancel_tag, _} -> raise "Batch returned while resolution was waiting"
    after
      0 -> :ok
    end

    true = Process.exit(caller, :kill)

    receive do
      {:DOWN, ^caller_monitor, :process, ^caller, :killed} -> :ok
    after
      5_000 -> raise "Batch caller did not stop"
    end

    receive do
      {:DOWN, ^resolver_monitor, :process, ^resolver, :killed} -> :ok
    after
      5_000 -> raise "Caller cancellation did not stop its resolver"
    end

    false = Process.alive?(caller)
    false = Process.alive?(resolver)

    receive do
      {:batch_returned, ^cancel_tag, _} -> raise "Cancelled batch released a result"
    after
      0 -> :ok
    end
  after
    Process.demonitor(resolver_monitor, [:flush])
  end
after
  Process.exit(caller, :kill)
  Process.demonitor(caller_monitor, [:flush])
end

{:ok, ^singles} = JWE.decrypt_many(items, policy)
IO.puts("Caller cancellation stopped the resolver; a fresh batch still decrypts")
```

## Release every caller-owned handle

```elixir
for handle <- [handle_a, handle_b] do
  :ok = Custody.release(handle)
end

{:ok, [
  {:error, %{reason: :decryption_failed, layer: :crypto}},
  {:error, %{reason: :decryption_failed, layer: :crypto}},
  {:error, %{reason: :decryption_failed, layer: :crypto}}
]} = JWE.decrypt_many(items, policy)

IO.puts("Released handles produce uniform positional crypto failures")
```

The API returns one final ordered list, with no partial batch plaintext. Successful decryption establishes recipient integrity, not sender identity or authorization. This local lifecycle exercise does not claim deployed-peer acceptance. See the [JOSE guide](../docs/guides/jose.md), [key custody](../docs/guides/key-custody.md), and [testing and evidence](../docs/guides/testing.md).
